@title CI Provider for Firmware and Cloud Repos
@subtitle Replacing Jenkins, Developer Experience at Ridgeback Robotics
@author Developer Experience

We run roughly 38000 build minutes a month for 120 engineers. Some jobs need ARM runners, and the hardware-in-the-loop lab needs self-hosted agents. Three providers are in the running: GitHub Actions, CircleCI and Buildkite.

## Comparison

table = Provider,Median build time (min),ARM runners,Self-hosted agents,Monthly cost; GitHub Actions,14.2,yes,yes,"$2,860"; CircleCI,11.8,yes,yes,"$3,420"; Buildkite,9.6,via our own agents,yes (required),"$2,150 plus our compute"

## Benchmarks

Median build time in minutes for our three reference pipelines on each provider.

data bench = Pipeline,GitHub Actions,CircleCI,Buildkite; firmware-build,21.4,17.9,13.2; cloud-api-tests,9.8,8.1,7.4; docs-site,3.1,2.7,2.9

chart bar bench y=GitHub Actions,CircleCI,Buildkite unit=min labels title="Median build time by pipeline"

## Pros and cons

### GitHub Actions

**Pros**

- Supports ARM runners and self-hosted agents.
- Lowest monthly cost of the two hosted options.

**Cons**

- Slowest median build time of the three.
- Firmware build takes 21.4 minutes.

### CircleCI

**Pros**

- Supports ARM runners and self-hosted agents.
- Faster than GitHub Actions on every reference pipeline.

**Cons**

- Highest monthly cost, at $3,420.
- Slower than Buildkite on firmware-build and cloud-api-tests.

### Buildkite

**Pros**

- Fastest median build time, at 9.6 minutes.
- Fastest on firmware-build and cloud-api-tests.

**Cons**

- Self-hosted agents are required, so we run the compute.
- Runner patching moves to the DevEx on-call.

## Recommendation

! Buildkite, because the hardware-in-the-loop lab needs self-hosted agents anyway.

!! Buildkite moves runner patching onto the DevEx on-call, estimated at 6 hours a month.
